`pre-registration.json` is here and hashes to something other than the
`pre_registration_sha256` the front-matter declares.

The endpoints a run was scored against are not the endpoints committed beside
it, which is the whole of what pre-registering them is for: a pre-registration
that can be edited after the numbers is a set of endpoints chosen to fit them.

Ruled 2026-09-14, and it is a FILE digest on purpose. Hashing the
pre-registration as a block inside `report.json` would have made
`check-results.py` serialise a sub-object to check it -- deciding key order,
separators and, because the block carries the attainable p floor and the budget
ladder, decimal formatting. That is a second canonicaliser of record data, and
one that disagrees with `diet` by a digit reports a mismatch indistinguishable
from tampering. A file has bytes, and `digest_of` already hashes bytes for every
other artefact in the directory.

The check is BICONDITIONAL: `2026-02-01-pre-registration-unpinned` is the other
half, a directory carrying the file and declaring no digest at all. Either half
alone would be a pin that can be escaped by dropping the other.
